  "summary": "An artistic tradition featuring intricate geometric and floral motifs used in carving and textile design",
  "description": "Okir art features flowing geometric patterns, arabesque designs, and stylized natural elements like the naga (dragon) and sarimanok (mythical bird), created through wood carving and textile weaving. These motifs reflect the Maranao's Islamic faith and their sophisticated cultural heritage, with designs symbolizing prosperity, protection, and cosmic harmony.",
  "culturalContext": "The origins of okir are pre-Islamic. They are believed to have originated from the much earlier okil or okil-okil decorative carving traditions of the Sama (Badjao) people, which are often highly individualistic and rectilinear. The Sama are master carvers, and they made lavish decorations on ritual animistic objects, grave markers (both in wood and stone), and their houseboats. These precursor forms of the okir designs can still be found in the art traditions of the Maranao in the basak (lowland) regions of Lake Lanao, and they contrast markedly from the later flowing okir designs.",
